---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
// Drafted for the operator to personalize — swap in your name, sectors, and the
// specific platforms you've used. Kept noindex until you've made it yours:
// remove `noindex={true}` below when the bio is final.
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Who writes this guide, and how it makes money." noindex={true}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
    <article class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>About this guide</h1>

      <p>
        I've spent more than ten years in institutional fundraising and grant
        work — writing the proposals, running the research, and sitting through
        the software demos this site now saves you from. Most of that time has
        been in and around education, where budgets are tight, boards ask hard
        questions, and a wrong software decision follows you for years.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site exists because the advice I could find online came almost
        entirely from two sources: vendors, and affiliate sites that had clearly
        never run a fundraising program. Neither will tell you that the CRM is
        the last decision, not the first; that a grassroots shop's best stack
        costs $0; or that a wealth screen without a gift officer to work it is
        the most expensive PDF in fundraising. Practitioners know these things.
        Vendors are structurally unable to say them.
      </p>

      <h2>How the guide works</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is organized by shop size, because that — not feature lists —
        is what actually determines what you should run. Every price carries a
        “last verified” date. Every recommendation names the constraint it
        depends on. And the free options are covered honestly alongside the paid
        ones, because a comparison that hides Zeffy or a library's free Candid
        access is a comparison you shouldn't trust.
      </p>

      <h2>How it makes money</h2>
      <p>
        Some outbound links are affiliate links, marked and disclosed. If you
        start a paid plan through one, this site may earn a commission at no
        cost to you. Several tools recommended here — including some
        recommended most strongly — pay nothing at all. The recommendation
        logic runs on fit; the links come after. Read the
        <a href="/disclosure/">full disclosure</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Questions, corrections, or a price that's gone stale?
        The fastest way to keep a guide like this honest is readers who call
        things out — please do.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
